WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.510
Film Courage: What does an outline look like?

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:04.840
Mark Sanderson, screenwriter and author: Well
for me on these assignments that I do they

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.230
are usually 10 or 12 pages.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:11.820
It’s the entire story, treatment and it’s
not just line by line.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.360
I mean there are some outlines that it’s
just a line literally.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:22.750
But for television they’re 8 acts so everything
has to be broken down but I write it as a

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.550
story so if you were to sit down and read
it, you’d be reading the story of the movie

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:33.020
which I think is essential to create, in fact
I’ve had friends that do this “Oh, I’m

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.180
just going to sit down and I have the vague
idea and I’m just going to write.”

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.489
And I’m like “You’ll be lost in the
barren wasteland of Act 2.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:45.550
With no water, you’ll be in the desert lost
and you’ll wonder how you’re going to

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.540
trudge through the 75-pages or whatever.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.899
I’m a huge advocate of starting outlines
before starting writing and I know it’s

00:00:54.899 --> 00:01:04.500
probably 50, 60, 70 percent of the work because
it makes the load a lot easier and you can

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.570
write a faster screenplay if you have an outline.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:14.000
Now that doesn’t say there is no room for
changes or improvising but if you don’t

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.110
have a solid roadmap going in…it’s almost
like a pre-draft of a first draft.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:24.380
And I’m not an advocate of what people say
the vomit draft or just spill it out.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.860
But I don’t have the luxury of spilling
it out on my assignment jobs, I don’t.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:35.220
I really have to turn in let’s say on a
ten scale turn in probably an eight…an eight

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:43.409
out of ten…because I’m now holding up
development and I’ve also done rewrites

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:51.630
on other screenwriter’s work/projects that
means that the script that they had went through

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.850
multiple drafts and still is not there.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:01.430
And they have a buyer, they have network who
is waiting on the script and so I’ve also

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:06.479
been hired to do rewrite job where I can come
in but it’s like a page one rewrite where

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.220
see the script that you have you can’t use
any of it, the names yes, the concept yes

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:17.140
but you basically…but that’s something
that I’ve learned how to do which is good

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:24.790
because there is a lot of rewrite work out
there and some writers look down on it.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:30.440
An example is that I took a rewrite job and
that ended up being three more jobs for the

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:36.440
same company because that opened the door
for them and they were thankful for that.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:45.260
But you never know what opportunity you’re
going to either turn down or accept.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:50.450
But back to your point about the outline,
I think it’s extremely important to do an

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.840
outline before your screenplay because it’s
easier to work out the problems there than

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.260
it is writing a complete first draft and just
having so many problems.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:05.170
And also it trains you like I said, like you’re
an olympic athlete, it trains you for the

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.860
time that you do have to do outlines because
I’m not allowed to write these assignments

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.510
without an outline that I have to create.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.040
So they just wont let me go to pages.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.260
They have other people involved who have to
okay it.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.050
So the outline is extremely important.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:23.170
Film Courage: In your book do you have any
examples of an actual outline?

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.190
I know sometimes viewers will ask “Where
do I find a copy of an outline?”

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.090
Mark Sanderson: I don’t.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.040
I’m sure you can find it online.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.030
But it’s not that mysterious.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:40.980
There is a step outline, there’s a one sheet
which is just a one sheet of paper which is

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:41.980
the concept.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.500
It’s sort of a short synopsis.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.300
There’s the log line which is even smaller
(two or three sentences).

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.630
So there are different steps and you have
to have each along the way because you have

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.200
the quick pitch “Hmmm…I’m not interested.”

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.510
Then you have the quick pitch and they go
“Hmmm?

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.940
I’m willing to read something (not the script
but I’ll ready the synopsis).

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:10.540
Then there are different steps as to how interested
they are to read what you want as the final

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:19.030
product, the final script is for them to read.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:23.680
A step outline, I’ve done outlines that
were 30 pages.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.840
I mean you triple it and you have the script.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.150
Everything had to be worked out because it
was very technical the way the film was going

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:39.400
to be shot from like an iPhone and from different
things and so really it had to be spelled

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:45.010
out because on the set you just can’t leave
it up to chance so.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:48.930
But after that point I could have written
a script in weeks because it was all figured

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.780
out, I’d figured it out already.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:56.000
And you want that outline for those dark periods
when you’re stuck.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.210
You don’t want to be stuck trying to figure
out plot points when you should be filling

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.210
pages.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.160
That’s the worst part, that’s the worst
place to be.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.080
Film Courage: How long does it usually take
you to write an outline?

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:18.850
Mark Sanderson: From once the concept is there,
like two weeks, working everyday.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:27.230
And then that usually ends up being 12-15
pages as the outline in my experience with

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:28.710
my producers.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:35.960
And that outline is then rewritten sometimes
two or three times, I get notes on the outline

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.610
before I’m allowed to go to the script.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:43.770
So the script is the final…once we okay
the outline, then go to pages.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:48.640
And that’s not to say they don’t change
their mind once the script is finished because

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.890
that’s the crazy part…

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.460
“Yeah but you agreed to it in the outline?”

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.200
But we built the house but now we don’t
kind of like the way it looks.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.840
You know we thought we did.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.310
So that happens, too.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:09.170
To be open and prepared for rewrites at all
stages it does allow you to write a faster

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.889
first draft is to have a solid outline.

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:16.130
Because for me I have to see the film in my
head before I can write it.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.620
And I know that I have a lot of problems ahead
of me if I can’t see it.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.720
I don’t lock in, like I’ve almost seen
the movie.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.830
Like when you watch a movie, you remember
it.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.090
I have to see it that way.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.240
And I know things are working.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.480
And you know the outline is good for living
with your characters.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.290
You get to live with them.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.760
You get to see how the movie is working or
doesn’t work before you sit down and write

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.380
that screenplay, which is building the house
(which is almost at the pre-blueprint) in

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.830
my opinion, that’s been my experience.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:53.990
But I know writers who just want to write
down a couple of lines and wing it.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:07:00.980
There are so many things that can go wrong
and why not turn in the most amazing first

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:01.980
draft you can?

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.130
I don’t see the problem with that.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:08.190
And like I say, you have to when you’re
doing assignment work.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:09.930
You want fewer drafts.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.930
You don’t want eight drafts because nothing
is working.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.150
You want to turn that in where they go “Wow!”

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.600
One of my assignments I did two drafts and
two polishes.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.600
We’re done!

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.600
That’s what they like.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.570
They don’t 5 [drafts] “You’re not getting
it…”

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:27.550
That’s where you get fired and they hire
someone else who can facilitate the notes

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.480
and get it moving.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.520
It’s creative but it’s also a business
at the time, unfortunately.

